  "_exec_summary": [
    {
      "ESSeqNum": 0,
      "FacilityID": 1000121122,
      "SummaryText": "US Magnesium LLC (US Mag) is a fully integrated primary magnesium producer located in a remote portion of Tooele County in Western Utah identified on maps as Rowley. US Mag employed up to about 450 individuals in a wide variety of jobs. The facility for magnesium production at Rowley operated for approximately 35 years. The magnesium operation derived its raw materials form the waters of the Great Salt Lake.   The manufacturing facility utilized a wide variety of chemical, electrochemical and pyro-metallurgical unit processes to produce high-grade primary and alloyed magnesium. In addition to magnesium, US Mag also produced chlorine as a co-product and other byproducts from its operations. \n\nIn September 2022, US Mag suspended operation of all of its magnesium production operations and laid-off over 200 employees. By mid-year 2023, US Mag had shipped all of its remaining liquified chlorine inventory (from its chlorine plant tanks and railcars) to customers. The only remaining chlorine at the plant is in 500-pound cylinders used to chlorinate water at the plant's water treatment plant that delivers potable water in the plant water system. If  US Mag resumes magnesium production, it will update this RMP to reflect any change in chlorine production and storage at the plant.  \n\nIn regards to this RMP 5-year updae, US Mag still has two chemicals in volumes that exceed the threshold quantities specified by the regulation.  Chlorine, which is stored and used onsite, is at maximum quantities equal to the 2,500 lb threshold value.  Titanium tetrachloride was used as a manufacturing aid to produce certain specialty magnesium products.   It was received in commercial liquid cylinders of 3,200 lbs, which exceeds the 2,500 lbs threshold. After suspension of magnesium production, one of the titanium terachloride cylinders remains on site in its stores inventory.\n\nUS Mag is committed to the safety of its employees as well as the safety of the surrounding community. It has taken th"
    },
    {
      "ESSeqNum": 1,
      "FacilityID": 1000121122,
      "SummaryText": "e necessary steps to prevent the accidental release of hazardous chemicals including those regulated by the regulation under which this document is submitted/resubmitted. To aid the prevention of accidental releases, US Mag has long had a published emergency response plan that addresses a wide range of contingencies involving on-site as well as off-site consequences should an accident occur. US Mags analyzed its production processes by formal Process Hazard Analysis methods utilizing a diversity of employee disciplines and perspectives to assure that the potential hazards are recognized and properly addressed.  US Mag has a system that addresses and manages changes to its equipment and processes in a controlled way that recognizes and minimizes the inherent hazards. US Mag has an extensive employee training program that deals with all facets involved of its operations.      The range of that training involves job safety, product quality, environmental responsibility, production efficiency, etc. This training system develops the necessary working knowledge to assist the prevention of accidental releases.     \n\nIn the last five years, there have been no accidental releases of regulated chemicals that have resulted in evacuation or property damage. Our records don't show any incident where there were off-site deaths, injuries, significant property or environmental damage that has occurred as the result of accidental release of either chlorine or titanium tetrachloride.\n\nUS Mag continues to be a member of the LEPC and submit annual Tier 2 reports to the State and the LEPC."
    }
  ]
